2.2 Reading the data
The accommodation series is read exactly as in Project 1. The exchange rate is downloaded from FRED the first time the document is rendered and saved as usdzar_monthly_fred.csv; later renders use the saved file. If the download fails, download the CSV from the FRED page above and save it under that name in the same folder as this file.


Code
# Accommodation (as in Project 1) -----------------------------------------
raw &lt;- read_excel(tourism_file)

nights_sold &lt;- raw |&gt;
  filter(H03 == "B64100") |&gt;
  select(starts_with("MO")) |&gt;
  pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = "code", values_to = "nights") |&gt;
  mutate(Month  = yearmonth(make_date(as.integer(substr(code, 5, 8)),
                                      as.integer(substr(code, 3, 4)), 1L)),
         nights = as.numeric(nights)) |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(nights)) |&gt;
  select(Month, nights) |&gt;
  as_tsibble(index = Month) |&gt;
  filter_index("2015 Jan" ~ .) |&gt;
  mutate(period = case_when(
    Month &lt;  covid_start ~ "Pre-COVID",
    Month &lt;= covid_end   ~ "COVID window",
    TRUE                 ~ "Post-COVID"
  ))

# Exchange rate -------------------------------------------------------------
if (!file.exists(zar_file)) {
  fred_url &lt;- paste0("https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=EXSFUS",
                     "&cosd=2015-01-01&coed=2026-06-01")
  download.file(fred_url, zar_file, mode = "wb")
}

zar &lt;- read.csv(zar_file, na.strings = c(".", ""))
names(zar) &lt;- c("date", "zar")
zar &lt;- zar |&gt;
  mutate(Month = yearmonth(as.Date(date)), zar = as.numeric(zar)) |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(zar)) |&gt;
  select(Month, zar)

nights_sold &lt;- nights_sold |&gt;
  left_join(zar, by = "Month") |&gt;
  mutate(log_zar = log(zar))

sum(is.na(nights_sold$zar))   # should be 0


[1] 0




Code
p_n &lt;- nights_sold |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(as.Date(Month), nights)) +
  annotate("rect", xmin = as.Date(covid_start), xmax = as.Date(covid_end),
           ymin = -Inf, ymax = Inf, alpha = 0.15, fill = "firebrick") +
  geom_line(colour = "steelblue4") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::label_comma()) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Nights sold (thousands)")

p_z &lt;- nights_sold |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(as.Date(Month), zar)) +
  annotate("rect", xmin = as.Date(covid_start), xmax = as.Date(covid_end),
           ymin = -Inf, ymax = Inf, alpha = 0.15, fill = "firebrick") +
  geom_line(colour = "darkorange3") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "ZAR per USD")

if (requireNamespace("patchwork", quietly = TRUE)) {
  patchwork::wrap_plots(p_n, p_z, ncol = 1)
} else {
  print(p_n); print(p_z)
}








Figure 1: Top: monthly stay unit nights sold (thousands), with the COVID window shaded. Bottom: monthly average rand per US dollar.





The full exploratory analysis of the accommodation series is in Project 1 and is not repeated here. The points that matter for this project are: the seasonal shape is very stable from year to year; the April 2020 collapse was followed by a recovery that took about two years, with setbacks in the later COVID waves; and the post-COVID level is still slightly below the pre-COVID level. The rand weakened over the period, with a sharp depreciation at the start of the pandemic (Figure 1), so the exchange rate and the COVID shock are partly confounded in 2020. This is one reason the exchange rate is added only after the shock has been modelled.

4.5 Residual diagnostics


Code
reg_aug &lt;- augment(fit_reg)

n_arma &lt;- tidy(fit_reg) |&gt;
  filter(grepl("^(ar|ma)[0-9]+$", term)) |&gt;
  count(.model, name = "dof")

reg_lb &lt;- reg_aug |&gt;
  as_tibble() |&gt;
  left_join(n_arma, by = ".model") |&gt;
  mutate(dof = coalesce(dof, 0L)) |&gt;
  group_by(.model) |&gt;
  summarise(lb_p = Box.test(.innov, lag = 24, type = "Ljung-Box",
                            fitdf = first(dof))$p.value,
            .groups = "drop")

lbp &lt;- function(m) reg_lb$lb_p[reg_lb$.model == m]




Code
fit_reg |&gt; select(Reg) |&gt; gg_tsresiduals(lag_max = 36)








Figure 5: Residual diagnostics for the regression model with ARIMA errors (without the exchange rate).





The Ljung and Box test on 24 lags gives p = 0.556 for the regression model and p = 0.544 for the model with the exchange rate. The residuals of the regression model are consistent with white noise and show no remaining seasonal structure, so the deterministic seasonal dummies are adequate. Unlike the Project 1 SARIMA fitted to the full data, the COVID months no longer dominate the residuals, because the shock is now explained by the intervention terms. The model passes step 5 and can be used to forecast.

3.3 STL decomposition


Code
nights_sold |&gt;
  model(STL(log(nights) ~ trend(window = 13) + season(window = "periodic"),
            robust = TRUE)) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  autoplot() +
  labs(x = NULL)








Figure 4: Robust STL decomposition of log nights sold, January 2015 to June 2026, with a fixed (periodic) seasonal component.





The log scale is used because the seasonal swings are roughly proportional to the level (they shrank when the level fell in 2020 and 2021), so a log transformation turns multiplicative seasonality into additive seasonality. On the pre-COVID data the strength of seasonality is 0.93 and the strength of trend is 0.64 (both measured from 0 to 1), so seasonality is the dominant feature. In Figure 4 the COVID collapse is absorbed almost entirely by the trend and the remainder, while the seasonal component is unaffected.
